{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1791184456,"nonce":"prLwq31QLXIxJ9kpzEJYxQaBwQw=","message":"**Owner's ruling (2026-10-05), on 5ace8f3. Supersedes the body where they conflict.**\n\n1. **Risk rules are written from the host shell only, like approval grants (47f67c8).** As built in 5ace8f3, any org member could `PUT /api/v1/remedy-risk-rules`, so one grant holder could write a one-approval rule and then approve a Remedy alone. That is the loophole ADR 0054 §4 closed for grants. Rules are now applied by `oto remedy-rules apply --org \u003cslug\u003e -f rules.yaml`, and the HTTP API and Settings screen are **read-only**: they show the rules with \"managed by `oto remedy-rules`\". No HTTP route writes a rule, which a test over the mounted router asserts.\n2. **The risk model's tokens count against the org's daily token budget** (ADR 0053 §6). When the budget is spent, the risk check does not run and the Remedy is recorded as needing two approvals, with that reason (fail closed, recorded).\n\n**Done when (added).** The PUT route is gone; the CLI applies a rules file atomically, and a malformed file changes nothing and exits non-zero, naming the problem; the Settings screen is read-only; risk-model tokens are summed into the day's spend; a test shows that a spent budget yields two approvals with a recorded reason; ADR 0054 §3 records both rulings.","files":null}]}